---
/**
 * Toolkit, English.
 *
 * The question this page answers is answered by SCANNING, which drives two
 * things that look like exceptions and are not:
 *
 *   1. The per-group paragraph is VISIBLE, not folded. It is the site's one
 *      documented exception to the level system: six clicks defeat a
 *      twenty-second scan, and that paragraph IS the added value over the
 *      technical section of the CV (information-architecture.md section 4.4).
 *      Measured in P-15d at 47 to 58 words per group, under the 60-word
 *      threshold that would have forced them back into folds.
 *
 *   2. No coral section rules here. On /what-i-do the six blocks are long
 *      enough that only one rule is ever in view; these groups are short, so
 *      several rules would share the visual field and break the one-coral-at-
 *      a-time rule of adr-002 section 3. Groups are separated by whitespace,
 *      and the accordion stays a signal exclusive to /what-i-do and /career.
 *
 * The six groups are written out rather than generated from a table: each one
 * has its own prose, two of them carry a glossary term or a link, and a table
 * of strings would have to grow a markup escape hatch on the first exception.
 * Only the chips, which really are uniform, come from data.
 *
 * Copy transcribed verbatim from web/content/en/toolkit.md.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Chip from '../components/Chip.astro';
import Term from '../components/Term.astro';

const CHIPS = {
  languages: ['C', 'Python', 'JavaScript', 'HTML', 'CSS', 'SQL', 'PHP'],
  mcus: [
    'Renesas RA / RX / RL78',
    'Infineon PSoC',
    'ST STM32 (F4, F7, H7)',
    'Freescale S08',
    'Microchip PIC',
    '8051-core MCUs (Silicon Labs, Atmel)',
  ],
  comms: [
    'UART',
    'SPI',
    'I2C',
    'QSPI',
    'SDRAM',
    'BLE',
    'Wi-Fi',
    'MQTT',
    'DALI',
    'DMX',
    'field buses',
  ],
  control: ['PID / PI loops', 'on-off control', 'FOPDT plant modeling'],
  safety: [
    'Class B (IEC 60730, IEC 60335)',
    'VDE',
    'UL',
    'CB Scheme',
    'risk analysis',
    'MISRA',
    'Polyspace',
  ],
  process: ['V-Model', 'Polarion', 'Git', 'Jira', 'Altium Designer', 'LTspice'],
};
---

<BaseLayout locale="en" current="toolkit" title="Toolkit | Franc Gaya" description="Languages, microcontrollers, communications, control, safety and standards, process and tools, and languages spoken. One rule decides what is on the list.">
  <PageHeader>
    <Fragment slot="title">Toolkit</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="lead">One rule decides what is on this page: <strong class="font-semibold text-ink-on-deep">I can take a task on it and deliver it.</strong> Having worked in a codebase that used something does not put it here, and neither does having read about it.</Fragment>
  </PageHeader>

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-shell px-6 pt-16">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-prose">
      <!-- Block 1. The rule is what turns a list into a claim: publishing the
           criterion disarms the inflated-inventory suspicion that every skills
           page invites. -->
      <p class="text-lead text-ink">That rule takes out more than it leaves in, which is the only reason a list like this is worth reading at all. There are no percentages, no star ratings, and no progress bars anywhere below, because a number would be exactly the claim the rule refuses to make.</p>

      <!-- Block 2. Six groups, one shape each: title, chips, visible paragraph.
           Before the 2026-08-06 revision this was two parallel lists with the
           same six titles, which is the shape the "a title appears once per
           page" rule was written to stop. -->
      <div class="mt-16 space-y-12">
        <section>
          <h2 class="text-h3 text-heading">Languages</h2>
          <ul class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
            {CHIPS.languages.map((c) => <li><Chip>{c}</Chip></li>)}
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-4 text-ink">C is where I have lived for sixteen years: <Term>bare metal</Term>, no operating system, on parts where a kilobyte is a real constraint. Python is the working language for everything around the product: test benches, data analysis, and plant modeling. The web stack is genuine but occasional, and it comes from a side project.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-h3 text-heading">Microcontrollers</h2>
          <ul class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
            {CHIPS.mcus.map((c) => <li><Chip>{c}</Chip></li>)}
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-4 text-ink">Nine vendors over sixteen years, from 8-bit parts written partly in assembler to Cortex-M7 driving a graphical framework. What carries across is never the family, it is the habits: read the errata before the datasheet, respect the peripheral mapping constraints when you fix the pinout, and assume the part will be second-sourced.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-h3 text-heading">Communications</h2>
          <ul class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
            {CHIPS.comms.map((c) => <li><Chip>{c}</Chip></li>)}
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-4 text-ink">Most of my work is boards talking to each other inside a single appliance, which is where the proprietary buses live, and displays talking to a microcontroller, which is where the bandwidth goes. The wireless side is newer and mostly serves connectivity modules and remote test benches, which is where MQTT earns its place.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-h3 text-heading">Control</h2>
          <ul class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
            {CHIPS.control.map((c) => <li><Chip>{c}</Chip></li>)}
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-4 text-ink">Thermal plants, mostly: slow, with real dead time, and with a response that changes with the operating mode and the size of the cavity. I model the plant before tuning the loop, because tuning on the real appliance costs hours per attempt and gives an answer that only holds for one configuration. <a href="/what-i-do/#firmware" class="text-link underline decoration-1 underline-offset-4">How that actually works</a>.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-h3 text-heading">Safety and standards</h2>
          <ul class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
            {CHIPS.safety.map((c) => <li><Chip>{c}</Chip></li>)}
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-4 text-ink">The hardest part of this inventory to acquire and to fake, because it is learned by defending a concept in front of a certifier rather than by reading the standard. MISRA and static analysis are what make the software side provable: in a certification, evidence and correctness are not the same claim.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-h3 text-heading">Process and tools</h2>
          <ul class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
            {CHIPS.process.map((c) => <li><Chip>{c}</Chip></li>)}
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-4 text-ink">The <Term>V-Model</Term> is how I think about a project rather than a box I tick: requirement, specification, design, and the test that proves each one. Altium and LTspice are here because I read and modify hardware and simulate the changes, not because I am the hardware designer.</p>
        </section>
      </div>

      <!-- Block 3. The LEVEL lives here; the CERTIFICATE lives in
           /career#certifications. Neither is duplicated in the other place,
           and that split is what governs the C1 when it arrives. -->
      <section class="mt-20 border-t border-rule pt-10">
        <h2 class="text-h2 text-heading">Languages I work in</h2>
        <ul class="evidence-list mt-6 text-ink">
          <li><strong class="font-semibold">Spanish</strong>, native.</li>
          <li><strong class="font-semibold">Catalan</strong>, native.</li>
          <li><strong class="font-semibold">English</strong>, working proficiency (CEFR B2/C1). Daily technical liaison with appliance manufacturers across Europe, Asia, and the Americas: meetings, specifications, counter-proposals, and field support.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
